ORGO.reg({"types":{"chair-both":{"id":"chair-both","title":"Draw both chairs, circle the more stable, name their relationship","exam":"Q8","points":100,"asks":"A disubstituted cyclohexane is drawn flat with wedges and dashes. Draw both chairs, circle the more stable one, and say whether the two chairs are the same, enantiomers or diastereomers.","know":[{"h":"The lecture's procedure: 1. draw the chairs. 2. decide which, if either, is more stable. 3. compare the chairs: same, enantiomers or diastereomers.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 3"},{"h":"A wedge means up and a dash means down. On a chair, up and down are shown by the direction of the bond: <b>no wedges or dashes when drawing chairs</b>.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 1"},{"h":"Axial bonds alternate up and down around the ring. If the axial bond at C1 points up, an up group is axial at C1, C3 and C5 and equatorial at C2, C4 and C6.","from":"standard"},{"h":"The <b>ring flip</b> swaps axial and equatorial at every carbon and leaves up and down alone. For unsubstituted cyclohexane K = 1.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 1"},{"h":"What the two chairs look like: cis-1,2, trans-1,3 and cis-1,4 have one axial and one equatorial group in each chair. trans-1,2, cis-1,3 and trans-1,4 have both equatorial in one chair and both axial in the other.","from":"standard"},{"h":"<b>Axial groups cost energy</b> (two gauche interactions with the ring). Cost in kcal/mol and K: methyl 1.74 (19.5); ethyl 1.79 (21.2); propyl 2.21 (43.4); isopropyl 2.61 (86.0); tert-butyl 5.5 (11,916).","from":"Table 5.3; Lecture 10/27, p. 2"},{"h":"More stable chair: both equatorial beats both axial. With one axial group in each chair, the chair with the <b>larger group equatorial</b> wins, even if only slightly (ethyl against methyl: 1.79 against 1.74).","from":"Exam key Q8(i)"},{"h":"<b>Same</b>: the flipped chair can be turned to lie exactly on the first one. <b>Enantiomers</b>: it cannot, but it is the mirror image. <b>Diastereomers</b>: neither.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 3 to 4"},{"h":"Chairs that are the same or enantiomers always have equal energy. So if the two chairs differ in energy, they are <b>diastereomers</b>.","from":"standard"},{"h":"<b>cis-1,2 with two identical groups</b> (the lecture's cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane): the chairs are equal in energy and are non-superimposable mirror images, conformational enantiomers. The molecule itself is achiral.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 4"},{"h":"<b>Identical groups, trans-1,3 or cis-1,4</b>: one axial and one equatorial in each chair, equal energy, and the flipped chair is the same chair turned around.","from":"standard"},{"h":"Skill: be able to draw a chair, add each group up or down on the right carbon, and draw its ring flip with every group on the same carbon and face.","from":"Lecture 10/27, p. 1 and 4"}],"method":["Read the flat drawing: which carbons, and is each group up (wedge) or down (dash)? Same face is cis.","Draw the first chair and place each group up or down on its carbon. See which are axial and which equatorial.","Draw the ring flip: every axial group becomes equatorial and the reverse, and up stays up.","Add up the axial costs in each chair and circle the cheaper one. If the costs are equal, say so.","If the energies differ, the chairs are diastereomers.","If the energies are equal, test whether one chair can be turned to lie on the other (same) or is only its mirror image (enantiomers)."],"pitfalls":[{"h":"Calling the chairs of cis-1-ethyl-2-methylcyclohexane equal or enantiomers because ethyl and methyl are nearly the same size. The groups differ, so the chairs are diastereomers."},{"h":"Calling every pair of equal-energy chairs the same. For cis-1,2 with identical groups they are enantiomers."},{"h":"Drawing wedges and dashes on a chair, or moving a group to the other face during the flip."},{"h":"Circling a chair when the two are equal in energy. Write that they are equal."},{"h":"Answering the relationship for two different molecules (cis against trans). The question is about the two chairs of one molecule."}],"links":{"read":["rings-2"]}}},"items":{"chair-both-001":{"id":"chair-both-001","type":"chair-both","role":"worked","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,2u:CH(CH3)2","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,2u:CH(CH3)2]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Methyl is on a wedge (up) and isopropyl is on a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 2. Same face: <b>cis</b>-1,2.","ask":{"q":"Are the two groups on the same face of the ring?","a":"Yes: cis."}},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl axial, C2 isopropyl equatorial. After the flip: C1 methyl equatorial, C2 isopropyl axial.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2u:CH(CH3)2|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2u:CH(CH3)2|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has one axial group, but not the same one. Axial methyl costs 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial isopropyl costs 2.61 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Circle the chair with the larger group, isopropyl, equatorial: it is better by 0.87 kcal/mol.","ask":{"q":"Which group costs more when it is axial?","a":"Isopropyl. So the better chair has it equatorial."}},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"In one chair methyl is axial, in the other isopropyl is axial. These shapes cannot be laid on top of each other and they are not mirror images. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy.","ask":{"q":"Can the flipped chair be laid exactly on the first one? If not, is it the mirror image?","a":"Neither. Different energies already rule both out: diastereomers."}}],"answer":"The chair with isopropyl equatorial (and methyl axial) is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"chair-both-002":{"id":"chair-both-002","type":"chair-both","role":"worked","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1d:Cl,2d:Cl","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1d:Cl,2d:Cl]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"The two Cl groups are on a dash (down) and a dash (down), on carbons 1 and 2. Same face: <b>cis</b>-1,2.","ask":{"q":"Are the two groups on the same face of the ring?","a":"Yes: cis."}},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 Cl equatorial, C2 Cl axial. After the flip: C1 Cl axial, C2 Cl equatorial.\n[[chair:1d:Cl,2d:Cl|A]]\n[[chair:1d:Cl,2d:Cl|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has exactly one axial Cl and one equatorial Cl. Same cost in both (about 0.53 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups)): equal in energy, K = 1. Circle neither.","ask":{"q":"How many axial groups does each chair have?","a":"One each, and it is the same group. The energies are equal."}},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Equal energy means the chairs are either the same or mirror images, so test it. Try to lay the flipped chair on the first one: it does not fit, however it is turned. But the mirror image of the first chair is exactly the flipped chair. Non-superimposable mirror images are <b>enantiomers</b>. The lecture calls them conformational enantiomers. They interconvert by the ring flip, which is why the molecule as a whole is achiral.","ask":{"q":"Can the flipped chair be laid exactly on the first one? If not, is it the mirror image?","a":"It cannot be superimposed, but it is the mirror image: enantiomers."}}],"answer":"The two chairs are equal in energy. The two chairs are enantiomers.","rule":"cis-1,2 with identical groups: equal energy, mirror-image chairs (enantiomers)."}},"chair-both-003":{"id":"chair-both-003","type":"chair-both","role":"worked","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,3d:CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,3d:CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"The two methyl groups are on a wedge (up) and a dash (down), on carbons 1 and 3. Opposite faces: <b>trans</b>-1,3.","ask":{"q":"Are the two groups on the same face of the ring?","a":"No: trans."}},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl axial, C3 methyl equatorial. After the flip: C1 methyl equatorial, C3 methyl axial.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,3d:CH3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH3,3d:CH3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has exactly one axial methyl and one equatorial methyl. Same cost in both (1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3)): equal in energy, K = 1. Circle neither.","ask":{"q":"How many axial groups does each chair have?","a":"One each, and it is the same group. The energies are equal."}},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Equal energy means the chairs are either the same or mirror images, so test it. Turn the flipped chair over like a pancake, about the line through C2 and C5. C1 and C3 trade places and it lands exactly on the first chair: one axial group, one equatorial group, on the same carbons and faces. The two chairs are the <b>same</b>.","ask":{"q":"Can the flipped chair be laid exactly on the first one? If not, is it the mirror image?","a":"Yes, after turning it. The chairs are the same."}}],"answer":"The two chairs are equal in energy. The two chairs are the same.","rule":"Identical groups, one axial and one equatorial at 1,3 (trans) or 1,4 (cis): the flip gives back the same chair."}},"chair-both-004":{"id":"chair-both-004","type":"chair-both","role":"worked","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"site","subs":"1u:C(CH3)3,4d:OH","given":"ring","ask":["cis-trans","stable","relationship"],"stem":"Is this isomer cis or trans? Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:C(CH3)3,4d:OH]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"tert-butyl is on a wedge (up) and OH is on a dash (down), on carbons 1 and 4. Opposite faces: <b>trans</b>-1,4.","ask":{"q":"Are the two groups on the same face of the ring?","a":"No: trans."}},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 tert-butyl axial, C4 OH axial. After the flip: C1 tert-butyl equatorial, C4 OH equatorial.\n[[chair:1u:C(CH3)3,4d:OH|A]]\n[[chair:1u:C(CH3)3,4d:OH|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"One chair has both groups equatorial and pays nothing. The other has both axial: 5.5 kcal/mol (Table 5.3) for tert-butyl plus about 0.9 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups) for OH. Circle the chair with both groups equatorial.","ask":{"q":"Which chair has an axial group to pay for?","a":"Only the one with both groups axial. The other has none."}},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"The two chairs are different shapes of the same molecule that cannot be laid on top of each other, and they are not mirror images: one has two equatorial groups, the other two axial groups. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy.","ask":{"q":"Can the flipped chair be laid exactly on the first one? If not, is it the mirror image?","a":"Neither. Different energies already rule both out: diastereomers."}}],"answer":"trans. The chair with both groups equatorial is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"chair-both-005":{"id":"chair-both-005","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":1,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,2d:CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,2d:CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"The two methyl groups are on a wedge (up) and a dash (down), on carbons 1 and 2. Opposite faces: <b>trans</b>-1,2."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl axial, C2 methyl axial. After the flip: C1 methyl equatorial, C2 methyl equatorial.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2d:CH3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2d:CH3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"One chair has both groups equatorial and pays nothing. The other has both axial: 2 x 1.74 = 3.48 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Circle the chair with both groups equatorial."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"The two chairs are different shapes of the same molecule that cannot be laid on top of each other, and they are not mirror images: one has two equatorial groups, the other two axial groups. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy. Each chair on its own is chiral, and so is the molecule, but the two chairs are not mirror images of each other."}],"answer":"The chair with both groups equatorial is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"chair-both-006":{"id":"chair-both-006","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":1,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,3u:CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,3u:CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"The two methyl groups are on a wedge (up) and a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 3. Same face: <b>cis</b>-1,3."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl axial, C3 methyl axial. After the flip: C1 methyl equatorial, C3 methyl equatorial.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,3u:CH3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH3,3u:CH3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"One chair has both groups equatorial and pays nothing. The other has both axial: 2 x 1.74 = 3.48 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). The two axial groups also crowd each other on the same face, so the real cost is higher still. Circle the chair with both groups equatorial."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"The two chairs are different shapes of the same molecule that cannot be laid on top of each other, and they are not mirror images: one has two equatorial groups, the other two axial groups. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy."}],"answer":"The chair with both groups equatorial is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"chair-both-007":{"id":"chair-both-007","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,4u:CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,4u:CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"The two methyl groups are on a wedge (up) and a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 4. Same face: <b>cis</b>-1,4."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl axial, C4 methyl equatorial. After the flip: C1 methyl equatorial, C4 methyl axial.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,4u:CH3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH3,4u:CH3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has exactly one axial methyl and one equatorial methyl. Same cost in both (1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3)): equal in energy, K = 1. Circle neither."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Equal energy means the chairs are either the same or mirror images, so test it. Spin the flipped chair half a turn about the vertical axis through the middle of the ring. C1 and C4 trade places and it lands exactly on the first chair: one axial group, one equatorial group, both still up or both still down. The two chairs are the <b>same</b>."}],"answer":"The two chairs are equal in energy. The two chairs are the same.","rule":"Identical groups, one axial and one equatorial at 1,3 (trans) or 1,4 (cis): the flip gives back the same chair."}},"chair-both-008":{"id":"chair-both-008","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":1,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,4d:CH3","given":"ring","ask":["cis-trans","stable","relationship"],"stem":"Is this isomer cis or trans? Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,4d:CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"The two methyl groups are on a wedge (up) and a dash (down), on carbons 1 and 4. Opposite faces: <b>trans</b>-1,4."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl axial, C4 methyl axial. After the flip: C1 methyl equatorial, C4 methyl equatorial.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,4d:CH3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH3,4d:CH3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"One chair has both groups equatorial and pays nothing. The other has both axial: 2 x 1.74 = 3.48 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Circle the chair with both groups equatorial."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"The two chairs are different shapes of the same molecule that cannot be laid on top of each other, and they are not mirror images: one has two equatorial groups, the other two axial groups. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy."}],"answer":"trans. The chair with both groups equatorial is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"chair-both-009":{"id":"chair-both-009","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH2CH3,2u:CH2CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH2CH3,2u:CH2CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"The two ethyl groups are on a wedge (up) and a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 2. Same face: <b>cis</b>-1,2."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 ethyl axial, C2 ethyl equatorial. After the flip: C1 ethyl equatorial, C2 ethyl axial.\n[[chair:1u:CH2CH3,2u:CH2CH3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH2CH3,2u:CH2CH3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has exactly one axial ethyl and one equatorial ethyl. Same cost in both (1.79 kcal/mol (Table 5.3)): equal in energy, K = 1. Circle neither."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Equal energy means the chairs are either the same or mirror images, so test it. Try to lay the flipped chair on the first one: it does not fit, however it is turned. But the mirror image of the first chair is exactly the flipped chair. Non-superimposable mirror images are <b>enantiomers</b>. The lecture calls them conformational enantiomers. They interconvert by the ring flip, which is why the molecule as a whole is achiral."}],"answer":"The two chairs are equal in energy. The two chairs are enantiomers.","rule":"cis-1,2 with identical groups: equal energy, mirror-image chairs (enantiomers)."}},"chair-both-010":{"id":"chair-both-010","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"site","subs":"1d:CH3,4d:C(CH3)3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1d:CH3,4d:C(CH3)3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Methyl is on a dash (down) and tert-butyl is on a dash (down), on carbons 1 and 4. Same face: <b>cis</b>-1,4."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl equatorial, C4 tert-butyl axial. After the flip: C1 methyl axial, C4 tert-butyl equatorial.\n[[chair:1d:CH3,4d:C(CH3)3|A]]\n[[chair:1d:CH3,4d:C(CH3)3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has one axial group, but not the same one. Axial tert-butyl costs 5.5 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial methyl costs 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Circle the chair with the larger group, tert-butyl, equatorial: it is better by 3.76 kcal/mol."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"In one chair tert-butyl is axial, in the other methyl is axial. These shapes cannot be laid on top of each other and they are not mirror images. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy. With K = 11,916 for tert-butyl, the chair with tert-butyl axial is hardly present at all."}],"answer":"The chair with tert-butyl equatorial (and methyl axial) is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"chair-both-011":{"id":"chair-both-011","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,3d:CH2CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,3d:CH2CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Methyl is on a wedge (up) and ethyl is on a dash (down), on carbons 1 and 3. Opposite faces: <b>trans</b>-1,3."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl axial, C3 ethyl equatorial. After the flip: C1 methyl equatorial, C3 ethyl axial.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,3d:CH2CH3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH3,3d:CH2CH3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has one axial group, but not the same one. Axial methyl costs 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial ethyl costs 1.79 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Circle the chair with the larger group, ethyl, equatorial: it is better by 0.05 kcal/mol, only slightly."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"In one chair methyl is axial, in the other ethyl is axial. These shapes cannot be laid on top of each other and they are not mirror images. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy. The trap is to call the chairs equal because methyl and ethyl are nearly the same size. Nearly is not equal: the groups are different, so the chairs are different."}],"answer":"The chair with ethyl equatorial (and methyl axial) is more stable, but only slightly. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"chair-both-012":{"id":"chair-both-012","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1d:Br,3u:Br","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1d:Br,3u:Br]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"The two Br groups are on a dash (down) and a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 3. Opposite faces: <b>trans</b>-1,3."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 Br equatorial, C3 Br axial. After the flip: C1 Br axial, C3 Br equatorial.\n[[chair:1d:Br,3u:Br|A]]\n[[chair:1d:Br,3u:Br|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has exactly one axial Br and one equatorial Br. Same cost in both (about 0.5 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups)): equal in energy, K = 1. Circle neither."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Equal energy means the chairs are either the same or mirror images, so test it. Turn the flipped chair over like a pancake, about the line through C2 and C5. C1 and C3 trade places and it lands exactly on the first chair: one axial group, one equatorial group, on the same carbons and faces. The two chairs are the <b>same</b>."}],"answer":"The two chairs are equal in energy. The two chairs are the same.","rule":"Identical groups, one axial and one equatorial at 1,3 (trans) or 1,4 (cis): the flip gives back the same chair."}},"chair-both-013":{"id":"chair-both-013","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"site","subs":"1u:OH,3u:CH3","given":"ring","ask":["cis-trans","stable","relationship"],"stem":"Is this isomer cis or trans? Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:OH,3u:CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"OH is on a wedge (up) and methyl is on a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 3. Same face: <b>cis</b>-1,3."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 OH axial, C3 methyl axial. After the flip: C1 OH equatorial, C3 methyl equatorial.\n[[chair:1u:OH,3u:CH3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:OH,3u:CH3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"One chair has both groups equatorial and pays nothing. The other has both axial: about 0.9 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups) for OH plus 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3) for methyl. The two axial groups also crowd each other on the same face, so the real cost is higher still. Circle the chair with both groups equatorial."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"The two chairs are different shapes of the same molecule that cannot be laid on top of each other, and they are not mirror images: one has two equatorial groups, the other two axial groups. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy."}],"answer":"cis. The chair with both groups equatorial is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"chair-both-014":{"id":"chair-both-014","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":1,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,2d:OH","given":"ring","ask":["cis-trans","stable","relationship"],"stem":"Is this isomer cis or trans? Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,2d:OH]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Methyl is on a wedge (up) and OH is on a dash (down), on carbons 1 and 2. Opposite faces: <b>trans</b>-1,2."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl axial, C2 OH axial. After the flip: C1 methyl equatorial, C2 OH equatorial.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2d:OH|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2d:OH|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"One chair has both groups equatorial and pays nothing. The other has both axial: 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3) for methyl plus about 0.9 kcal/mol (a literature value: the lecture table lists only alkyl groups) for OH. Circle the chair with both groups equatorial."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"The two chairs are different shapes of the same molecule that cannot be laid on top of each other, and they are not mirror images: one has two equatorial groups, the other two axial groups. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy."}],"answer":"trans. The chair with both groups equatorial is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"chair-both-015":{"id":"chair-both-015","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH2CH3,4u:CH2CH3","given":"ring","ask":["cis-trans","stable","relationship"],"stem":"Is this isomer cis or trans? Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH2CH3,4u:CH2CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"The two ethyl groups are on a wedge (up) and a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 4. Same face: <b>cis</b>-1,4."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 ethyl axial, C4 ethyl equatorial. After the flip: C1 ethyl equatorial, C4 ethyl axial.\n[[chair:1u:CH2CH3,4u:CH2CH3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH2CH3,4u:CH2CH3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has exactly one axial ethyl and one equatorial ethyl. Same cost in both (1.79 kcal/mol (Table 5.3)): equal in energy, K = 1. Circle neither."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Equal energy means the chairs are either the same or mirror images, so test it. Spin the flipped chair half a turn about the vertical axis through the middle of the ring. C1 and C4 trade places and it lands exactly on the first chair: one axial group, one equatorial group, both still up or both still down. The two chairs are the <b>same</b>."}],"answer":"cis. The two chairs are equal in energy. The two chairs are the same.","rule":"Identical groups, one axial and one equatorial at 1,3 (trans) or 1,4 (cis): the flip gives back the same chair."}},"chair-both-016":{"id":"chair-both-016","type":"chair-both","role":"practice","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"site","subs":"1u:CH3,2u:C(CH3)3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,2u:C(CH3)3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Methyl is on a wedge (up) and tert-butyl is on a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 2. Same face: <b>cis</b>-1,2."},{"t":"Draw the two chairs","h":"Put each group on its carbon, up or down as in the flat drawing, then flip the ring: axial and equatorial swap, up and down do not. No wedges on a chair. First chair: C1 methyl axial, C2 tert-butyl equatorial. After the flip: C1 methyl equatorial, C2 tert-butyl axial.\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2u:C(CH3)3|A]]\n[[chair:1u:CH3,2u:C(CH3)3|B]]"},{"t":"Compare the energies","h":"Each chair has one axial group, but not the same one. Axial methyl costs 1.74 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Axial tert-butyl costs 5.5 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). Circle the chair with the larger group, tert-butyl, equatorial: it is better by 3.76 kcal/mol."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"In one chair methyl is axial, in the other tert-butyl is axial. These shapes cannot be laid on top of each other and they are not mirror images. They are <b>diastereomers</b>. Quick test: chairs with different energies can only be diastereomers, because identical shapes and mirror images always have the same energy."}],"answer":"The chair with tert-butyl equatorial (and methyl axial) is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"Chairs of different energy are diastereomers. Larger group equatorial; both equatorial beats both axial."}},"exam-q8-i":{"id":"exam-q8-i","type":"chair-both","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q8(i)","subs":"1u:CH3,2u:CH2CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,2u:CH2CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Both groups are on wedges: both up. This is cis-1-ethyl-2-methylcyclohexane."},{"t":"Draw the chairs","h":"The lecture's procedure, step 1. Two up groups on neighbouring carbons are one axial and one equatorial. Chair one: {{CH3}} axial, ethyl equatorial. The flip: {{CH3}} equatorial, ethyl axial. No wedges or dashes on a chair."},{"t":"Compare energies","h":"Step 2. Each chair has one axial group. An axial methyl costs 1.74 kcal/mol, an axial ethyl 1.79 (Table 5.3). The chair with the larger group, ethyl, equatorial is more stable, but only by about 0.05 kcal/mol."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Step 3. The chairs are not identical and they are not mirror images of each other: one has an axial methyl and the other an axial ethyl. Stereoisomers that are not mirror images are diastereomers."}],"answer":"The chair with ethyl equatorial and methyl axial is slightly more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"cis-1,2 with different groups: larger group equatorial, chairs are diastereomers."}},"exam-q8-ii":{"id":"exam-q8-ii","type":"chair-both","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":2,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q8(ii)","subs":"1d:CH3,4u:CH(CH3)2","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1d:CH3,4u:CH(CH3)2]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"{{CH3}} on a dash (down), isopropyl on a wedge (up), on carbons 1 and 4: trans-1-isopropyl-4-methylcyclohexane."},{"t":"Draw the chairs","h":"Carbons 1 and 4 have axial bonds pointing in opposite directions. One group down on C1 and one up on C4 are therefore both axial in one chair and both equatorial in the other."},{"t":"Compare energies","h":"The chair with both groups axial pays for both: 1.74 + 2.61 = 4.35 kcal/mol (Table 5.3). The chair with both equatorial pays nothing. It is far more stable."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"Different shapes, different energies, not mirror images: diastereomers."}],"answer":"The chair with both groups equatorial is more stable. The two chairs are diastereomers.","rule":"trans-1,4: both equatorial or both axial. Both equatorial wins."}},"exam-q8-iii":{"id":"exam-q8-iii","type":"chair-both","role":"exam","kind":"chair","diff":3,"src":"FA24 Exam 2, Q8(iii)","subs":"1u:CH3,2u:CH3","given":"ring","ask":["stable","relationship"],"stem":"Draw both chair conformations. Circle the more stable one, or say that they are equal. What is the relationship between the two chairs?\n[[ring:1u:CH3,2u:CH3]]","solution":{"steps":[{"t":"Read the faces","h":"Both methyls on wedges: cis-1,2-dimethylcyclohexane. The lecture worked this exact molecule on 10/27."},{"t":"Draw the chairs","h":"Chair one: the C1 methyl axial, the C2 methyl equatorial. The flip: C1 equatorial, C2 axial."},{"t":"Compare energies","h":"Each chair has exactly one axial methyl (1.74 kcal/mol) and one equatorial methyl. Same energy: K = 1. Neither is circled."},{"t":"Name the relationship","h":"The chairs are not superimposable, but one is the mirror image of the other: the lecture calls them conformational enantiomers. They interconvert by the ring flip, which is why the molecule as a whole is achiral."}],"answer":"The two chairs are equal in energy. They are enantiomers (non-superimposable mirror images).","rule":"cis-1,2 with identical groups: equal energy, mirror-image chairs."}}},"mols":{}});
